2025-11-16 15:53:58,587 INFO [utils.user_manager] Created new user: good, that payload is **exactly** the kind of thing we needed to see – and it also explains why your current calls are blowing up.  short answer: yes, this is basically what you need to send – but your code almost certainly is **not** building `fieldmap` like this right now. you’re using `pos_id=703` / `0` etc as keys, which is wrong. you need to:  * use these short ids (`"06znb"`, `"05nzu"`…) as the **keys**. * use `{"posid": "...", "stid": "1|2"}` as the **values**. * send the **full map** (all slots), not just the two changed slots. * use `rosterlimitperiod`, not some custom `period` field.  let’s break it down and then i’ll show you what your python needs to look like.  ---  ## what this payload tells us  here’s your payload, formatted:  ```json {   "msgs": [     {       "method": "confirmorexecuteteamrosterchanges",       "data": {         "rosterlimitperiod": 12,         "fantasyteamid": "fhmidjjjmcm8dnys",         "daily": false,         "adminmode": false,         "confirm": true,         "applytofutureperiods": true,         "fieldmap": {           "06znb": {"posid": "704", "stid": "1"},           "06v6n": {"posid": "703", "stid": "1"},           "05nzu": {"posid": "703", "stid": "1"},           "06v0g": {"posid": "703", "stid": "1"},           "0635j": {"posid": "703", "stid": "1"},           "05gcr": {"posid": "702", "stid": "1"},           "0642l": {"posid": "702", "stid": "1"},           "06fup": {"posid": "702", "stid": "1"},           "03rxs": {"posid": "702", "stid": "1"},           "06zbz": {"posid": "701", "stid": "1"},           "068y0": {"posid": "701", "stid": "1"},           "04tm0": {"posid": "703", "stid": "2"},           "03rzp": {"posid": "703", "stid": "2"},           "0700p": {"posid": "703", "stid": "2"},           "04qfm": {"posid": "702", "stid": "2"},           "05o6h": {"posid": "701", "stid": "2"}         }       }     }   ],   "uiv": 3,   "refurl": "https://www.fantrax.com/fantasy/league/10col873mcm62cqv/team/roster",   "dt": 0,   "at": 0,   "av": "0.0",   "tz": "america/los_angeles",   "v": "177.2.1" } ```  key points:  1. **the keys** in `fieldmap` (`"06znb"`, `"06v6n"`, …) are **slot/row ids**, not `pos_id`:     * these are *per-roster-row identifiers* – each corresponds to “one player in one lineup slot.”    * these are what you must use as the keys in your `fieldmap`.  2. **the values** are small objects:     * `posid`: `"701"`, `"702"`, `"703"`, `"704"` – position codes.    * `stid`: `"1"` or `"2"` – likely starter vs bench/reserve:       * `"1"` = starting xi slot.      * `"2"` = bench/reserve slot.  3. **there is no playerid in this payload.**     * the player is implicitly tied to the slot id.    * when you “swap” in the ui, fantrax is just changing the `stid` (and possibly posid), not remapping players by id in this call.  4. **it’s a full map**, not partial:     * every roster row is present – all starters + all reserves.    * your previous “just map out / in players via pos_id=703 and 0” will absolutely cause unexpected_error.  5. **`rosterlimitperiod` is set to 12**, not some `period` field.     * for this method, you should mirror that exact field name and semantics.  so yes: this shape is what your python needs to mimic.  ---  ## what your code needs to do differently  ### 1. stop using `pos_id` and `0` as fieldmap keys  your logs showed:  > out=lucas digne (pos_id=703) → bench(0), in=hartman (pos_id=0) → lineup(703)  that implies something like:  ```python field_map = {     "703": { ... },  # or "703": "hartman_id"     "0": { ... }, } ```  this is wrong. `703` is a **position code**, not “the slot where digne lives.”  you need to:  * fetch the roster rows, * for each row get its **slot id** (those `"06znb"` / `"05nzu"` strings), * build the full `fieldmap` keyed by *those* ids.  rough sketch:  ```python def build_field_map_from_roster(roster_rows):     """     roster_rows: list of dicts, each with something like:       {         "slotid": "06znb",       # or "id", "fieldid", etc.         "posid": "703",         "stid": "1"              # 1=starter, 2=bench         # plus other stuff (playerid, name, etc.)       }     """     field_map = {}     for row in roster_rows:         slot_id = row["slotid"]          # ← must match the keys like "06znb"         pos_id = str(row["posid"])         st_id = str(row["stid"])         field_map[slot_id] = {"posid": pos_id, "stid": st_id}     return field_map ```  then to swap digne ↔ hartman:  ```python def apply_swap(field_map, roster_rows, out_player_id, in_player_id):     # find slots by playerid     out_slot_id = none     in_slot_id = none     for row in roster_rows:         slot_id = row["slotid"]         if row["playerid"] == out_player_id:             out_slot_id = slot_id         elif row["playerid"] == in_player_id:             in_slot_id = slot_id      if out_slot_id is none or in_slot_id is none:         raise valueerror("could not find slot for one of the players")      # swap stid values between those slots     field_map[out_slot_id]["stid"], field_map[in_slot_id]["stid"] = (         field_map[in_slot_id]["stid"],         field_map[out_slot_id]["stid"],     )      # posid will often remain the same if both are def, but you can     # keep it as-is unless your league uses weird flex logic.     return field_map ```  then send that full `fieldmap` in the payload.  ---  ### 2. mirror the rest of the envelope exactly  use the same shape you captured from the ui:  ```python payload = {     "msgs": [         {             "method": "confirmorexecuteteamrosterchanges",             "data": {                 "rosterlimitperiod": current_period,           # 12                 "fantasyteamid": fantasy_team_id,              # "fhmidjjjmcm8dnys"                 "daily": false,                 "adminmode": false,                 "confirm": true,                               # see below                 "applytofutureperiods": true,                  # or false, mirror ui                 "fieldmap": field_map,                         # full, with slot ids             },         }     ],     "uiv": 3,     "refurl": f"https://www.fantrax.com/fantasy/league/{league_id}/team/roster",     "dt": 0,     "at": 0,     "av": "0.0",     "tz": "america/los_angeles",     "v": "177.2.1", } ```  on `confirm` vs `execute`:  * check devtools to see if the ui sends:    * one call with `confirm: true`, `applytofutureperiods: ?`, or   * two separate calls (one confirm, one execute). * whatever sequence it uses, **copy that**. if your captured call is from the successful swap and there’s only one call, just do that.  the critical bit for your bug is the `fieldmap` structure and `rosterlimitperiod`, not so much confirm/execute semantics.  ---  ### 3. why you were getting `unexpected_error` before  putting it together:  * you were telling fantrax: “move players using slot ids 703 and 0”. * the server is expecting slot ids like `"06znb"`, `"04tm0"`, etc. * so from the backend’s perspective, your `fieldmap` is garbage:    * it references slots that don’t exist.   * or leaves required slots unspecified if you were sending a partial map.  it can’t reconcile that with any valid lineup, so it throws `unexpected_error` at both confirm and execute.  once you:  * use the actual slot ids as keys, * send the full map, * and keep `rosterlimitperiod` / flags mirroring the ui,  that error should go away for digne ↔ hartman and all similar swaps.  ---  ## what i’d do next, concretely  1. **log what your code is currently sending** for confirm/execute. 2. make its `fieldmap` look **identical** in shape to the browser payload:     * keys: slotids like `"06znb"`.    * values: `{"posid": "...", "stid": "..."}` strings.    * full lineup, not just two entries. 3. keep `rosterlimitperiod`, `daily`, `adminmode`, `confirm`, `applytofutureperiods` exactly as you saw in devtools for that swap.  if you paste your current `execute_changes()` implementation, i can rewrite it to build this exact `fieldmap` from your roster response so you stop hitting this wall. (ID: 40aa0bf13ddf3ac1)
